Good question — let me be concrete about what each preset actually contains and why it needs to exist.

---

## What a preset IS

A preset is a named bundle of configuration that encodes knowledge about a specific library. Critically, **presets and auto-detection are the same thing** — when auto-detection finds `@mui/material` in `package.json`, it applies the MUI preset automatically. Explicit presets in `docgen.config.ts` exist for when you need to override auto-detection, use a library we don't recognize, or want an auditable config.

```ts
presets: [presets.mui()]  // explicit = same as what auto-detection would apply
```

---

## Preset anatomy

Every preset has two sides:

```ts
interface Preset {
  name: string
  rust: {                          // serialized to JSON → NAPI → Rust resolver
    variantFunctions?: string[]    
    knownTypeOverrides?: Record<string, KnownTypeOverride>
    extraBuiltins?: string[]       // added to is_react_builtin()
  }
  ts: {                            // stays in TS, runs after NAPI returns
    propFilter?: PropFilter        // composed with user's propFilter
    transformComponent?: (c: ComponentEntry) => ComponentEntry
    notableProps?: Partial<Record<HtmlElement, string[]>>
  }
}
```

---

## The presets we'd ship — concrete value

### `presets.shadcn()`

**Auto-activates when:** `@radix-ui/react-slot` is installed (the signal that shadcn components are present).

**What it adds:**
```ts
rust: {
  variantFunctions: ['cva'],   // already default, but explicit
}
ts: {
  propFilter: (prop) => {
    // asChild is always a component-owned prop, never filtered
    if (prop.name === 'asChild') return true
    return true  // defer to user's filter for everything else
  },
  notableProps: {
    button: ['className', 'variant', 'size', 'asChild', 'disabled', 'onClick'],
    input: ['className', 'value', 'defaultValue', 'placeholder', 'disabled'],
    div: ['className'],
  }
}
```

**Without it:** `asChild` might be filtered out by the standard propFilter (its parent is `@radix-ui/react-slot`, which is in `node_modules`). `className` might not appear in notable props.

---

### `presets.mui()`

The most important preset — MUI is the hardest case and the most common reason teams switch from RDT.

**Auto-activates when:** `@mui/material` or `@mui/base` detected.

**What it adds:**
```ts
rust: {
  knownTypeOverrides: {
    // These are MUI internal types that should never expand
    'classes': { kind: 'opaque', label: 'CSS class overrides per slot' },
    'slotProps': { kind: 'opaque', label: 'Props forwarded to internal component slots' },
    'ownerState': { kind: 'skip' },           // internal, never user-facing
    'OwnerStateResolver': { kind: 'skip' },
    // SxProps and OverridableStringUnion already in built-in known.rs
  }
}
ts: {
  propFilter: (prop) => {
    if (prop.name === 'ownerState') return false
    if (prop.name === 'classes' && prop.parent?.fileName.includes('node_modules')) return false
    // Always surface these even if from node_modules
    if (['sx', 'component', 'variant', 'color', 'size'].includes(prop.name)) return true
    return true  // defer to user
  },
  notableProps: {
    button: ['onClick', 'variant', 'color', 'size', 'disabled', 'loading',
             'startIcon', 'endIcon', 'fullWidth', 'href', 'sx'],
  },
  transformComponent: (comp) => {
    // Rename `component` prop description to explain polymorphism
    if (comp.props['component']) {
      comp.props['component'] = {
        ...comp.props['component'],
        description: comp.props['component'].description 
          || 'The component used for the root node. Can be a string (HTML element) or a component.',
      }
    }
    return comp
  }
}
```

**Without it:** `classes` object (50+ keys) expands into noise. `ownerState` appears in every component. `sx` might be filtered by propFilter since it comes from node_modules. `slotProps` becomes an unreadable object.

---

### `presets.chakra()`

**Auto-activates when:** `@chakra-ui/react` or `@ark-ui/react` detected.

**The key problem:** Chakra's `SystemProps` (their style system) adds ~200 CSS-property props (`mt`, `mb`, `px`, `color`, `bg`, etc.) to every component. This is worse than HTML attribute noise — it's Chakra-specific noise.

**What it adds:**
```ts
rust: {
  knownTypeOverrides: {
    'SystemProps': { kind: 'opaque', label: 'Chakra style system props' },
    'StyleProps': { kind: 'opaque', label: 'Chakra style system props' },
    'ChakraStyleProps': { kind: 'opaque', label: 'Chakra style system props' },
    'ResponsiveValue': { kind: 'opaque', label: 'Responsive style value' },
    // HTMLChakraProps and ThemingProps already in built-in known.rs
  }
}
ts: {
  notableProps: {
    button: ['onClick', 'isDisabled', 'isLoading', 'leftIcon', 'rightIcon',
             'variant', 'colorScheme', 'size', 'loadingText', 'spinner'],
  },
  transformComponent: (comp) => {
    // Collapse all SystemProps into a single synthetic prop
    const styleProps = Object.keys(comp.props).filter(k => isChakraStyleProp(k))
    if (styleProps.length > 10) {
      styleProps.forEach(k => delete comp.props[k])
      comp.props['__styleProps'] = {
        name: '__styleProps',
        description: `Chakra style system props (${styleProps.length} props: mt, mb, px, color, bg, ...)`,
        type: { kind: 'opaque', raw: 'ChakraStyleProps' },
        required: false,
      }
    }
    return comp
  }
}
```

**Without it:** Every Chakra component shows 200+ style system props alongside the actual API props. Unusable.

---

### `presets.mantine()`

**Auto-activates when:** `@mantine/core` detected.

**The key problem:** Mantine uses `StylesApiProps` which gives components `classNames`, `styles`, `unstyled`, and `vars` — all of which accept typed objects keyed by internal slot names that are meaningless to end users.

**What it adds:**
```ts
rust: {
  knownTypeOverrides: {
    // StylesApiProps already in known.rs
    'PolymorphicFactory': { kind: 'skip' },  // internal factory type
    'FactoryPayload': { kind: 'skip' },
    'CSSProperties': { kind: 'alias', argIndex: -1 },  // → React.CSSProperties
  }
  extraBuiltins: ['MantineColor', 'MantineSize', 'MantineRadius', 'MantineSpacing'],
}
ts: {
  notableProps: {
    button: ['onClick', 'variant', 'color', 'size', 'radius', 'disabled',
             'loading', 'leftSection', 'rightSection', 'fullWidth', 'justify'],
  },
  transformComponent: (comp) => {
    // MantineColor, MantineSize etc. are extensible string unions
    // Add description to help users understand they're theme-aware
    for (const [name, prop] of Object.entries(comp.props)) {
      if (prop.type.kind === 'named' && ['MantineColor','MantineSize','MantineRadius'].includes(prop.type.name)) {
        comp.props[name] = { ...prop, description: prop.description || `${prop.type.name} — value from your Mantine theme` }
      }
    }
    return comp
  }
}
```

---

### `presets.reactAria()`

The most different preset — React Aria uses ARIA-first naming and render props patterns that are unlike any other library.

**Auto-activates when:** `react-aria-components` detected (note: different from `react-aria` hooks).

**What it adds:**
```ts
rust: {
  extraBuiltins: [
    // These are RAC base types — terminal, don't try to expand
    'AriaButtonProps', 'AriaCheckboxProps', 'AriaTextFieldProps',
    'AriaSelectProps', 'AriaMenuProps', 'AriaDialogProps',
    'FocusableProps', 'HoverProps', 'PressProps', 'FocusEvents',
    'KeyboardEvents', 'DOMAttributes',  // RAC's, not React's
  ],
  knownTypeOverrides: {
    'Key': { kind: 'primitive', as: 'string | number' },
    'Selection': { kind: 'union', members: ['"all"', 'Set<Key>'] },
    'DisabledKeys': { kind: 'primitive', as: 'Set<Key>' },
    'ItemElement': { kind: 'skip' },    // internal collection type
    'CollectionElement': { kind: 'skip' },
  }
}
ts: {
  notableProps: {
    button: ['onPress', 'isDisabled', 'excludeFromTabOrder', 'autoFocus',
             'onFocus', 'onBlur', 'onHoverStart', 'onHoverEnd', 'children'],
    input: ['onChange', 'value', 'defaultValue', 'isDisabled', 'isReadOnly',
            'isRequired', 'isInvalid', 'autoFocus', 'placeholder', 'label'],
  },
  transformComponent: (comp) => {
    // Improve display names for boolean `is*` props
    for (const [name, prop] of Object.entries(comp.props)) {
      if (name.startsWith('is') && prop.type.kind === 'boolean') {
        const readable = name.replace(/^is/, '').replace(/([A-Z])/g, ' $1').trim()
        comp.props[name] = {
          ...prop,
          description: prop.description || readable,
        }
      }
    }
    return comp
  }
}
```

**Without it:** `onPress` doesn't appear in notable props (it looks like a custom event handler, not THE primary interaction). `isDisabled` renders as "Is Disabled" not "Disabled". RAC's internal collection types expand into noise. `Key`, `Selection` types are unreadable raw TypeScript.

---

### `presets.panda()`

**Auto-activates when:** `panda.config.ts` exists OR `@pandacss/dev` detected.

```ts
rust: {
  variantFunctions: ['defineRecipe', 'recipe', 'defineSlotRecipe'],
  knownTypeOverrides: {
    'RecipeVariantProps': { kind: 'alias', argIndex: 0 },  // → same as VariantProps
    'SystemStyleObject': { kind: 'opaque', label: 'PandaCSS style object' },
    'CssProperties': { kind: 'alias', argIndex: -1 },       // → React.CSSProperties
    'SlotRecipeVariantProps': { kind: 'opaque', label: 'Slot recipe variant props' },
  }
}
```

**The critical extra step:** The preset also adds the detected `styled-system/` dir to parse paths. Without this, `RecipeVariantProps<typeof buttonRecipe>` can't resolve because the recipe's generated types live in `styled-system/recipes/`. This is automatic when `pandaOutdir` is detected.

---

### `presets.styledComponents()` / `presets.emotion()`

These need **extractor changes**, not just config. Styled components produce components the current extractor won't detect:

```ts
const Button = styled.button<ButtonProps>`...`         // tagged template
const Input = styled(BaseInput)<{ hasError: boolean }>`` // composition
const Card = emotion.div<CardProps>``
```

The preset bundles the extractor config AND documents that styled component detection is active:

```ts
rust: {
  detectStyledComponents: true,  // new flag in PipelineOptions
  knownTypeOverrides: {
    'StyledComponentProps': { kind: 'alias', argIndex: 0 },
    'CSSObject': { kind: 'opaque', label: 'CSS-in-JS style object' },
    'Interpolation': { kind: 'opaque', label: 'CSS-in-JS interpolation' },
  }
}
```

**Without it:** Every styled component is invisible to our extractor. For teams on styled-components/emotion this is a complete non-starter.

