## Concrete Patterns From The Fixtures

### Pattern 1 — `Omit<T, K>` in extends (Chakra)
```ts
// Chakra Button.d.ts line 47
type HTMLChakraProps<Tag extends React.ElementType> =
  Omit<React.ComponentPropsWithoutRef<Tag>, "color"> &  // ← Omit in intersection
    ChakraStyleProps & { as?: React.ElementType }
```
The `Omit` is *inside* an intersection *inside* a type alias. Our current `CollectedTypeAlias::Omit` only handles `type X = Omit<Base, K>` at the top level. This nested form isn't covered.

### Pattern 2 — Discriminated union of full interfaces (MUI TextField)
```ts
// TextField.d.ts lines 237-240
export type TextFieldProps =
  | StandardTextFieldProps   // has variant: "standard"
  | FilledTextFieldProps     // has variant: "filled"
  | OutlinedTextFieldProps   // has variant: "outlined"
```
Each member is a complete interface with 30+ props. The discriminant is `variant`. What should docgen show? We can't blindly merge (conflicting `variant` types), and we can't show three separate prop tables.

### Pattern 3 — Indexed access types (MUI + Mantine + Chakra)
```ts
// MUI Button.d.ts line 84
tabIndex?: NonNullable<React.HTMLAttributes<HTMLElement>["tabIndex"]>

// Mantine Button.d.ts line 201
justify?: React.CSSProperties["justifyContent"]

// Chakra Button.d.ts lines 28-34
px?: React.CSSProperties["paddingLeft"]
py?: React.CSSProperties["paddingTop"]
```
Extremely common. `Type["key"]` extracts the type of a property. We currently store these as raw strings like `"React.CSSProperties[\"justifyContent\"]"` and the resolver has no idea what to do.

### Pattern 4 — Template literal types (Mantine)
```ts
// Mantine Button.d.ts line 150
size?: MantineSize | `compact-${MantineSize}`
// Where MantineSize = "xs" | "sm" | "md" | "lg" | "xl"
// → should expand to: "xs"|"sm"|"md"|"lg"|"xl"|"compact-xs"|"compact-sm"|...
```
Fully statically resolvable when both sides are string literal unions.

### Pattern 5 — `(string & {})` trick (Mantine)
```ts
// Mantine Button.d.ts line 42
export type MantineRadius = MantineSize | (string & {}) | number
```
A TypeScript narrowing trick meaning "string but not assignable to the literal union above". For docgen, `(string & {})` = `string`. We should normalize this.

### Pattern 6 — Render prop union (React Aria)
```ts
// Button.d.ts lines 54, 131, 132
children?: ReactNode | ((renderProps: ButtonRenderProps) => ReactNode)
className?: string | ((renderProps: ButtonRenderProps) => string)
style?: CSSProperties | ((renderProps: ButtonRenderProps) => CSSProperties)
```
A union of a value type and a function returning that value. Pattern: `T | ((state: S) => T)`. We should recognize this as `RenderPropType` and display it meaningfully.

### Pattern 7 — ARIA attribute mixed unions (React Aria)
```ts
// Button.d.ts lines 119-124
"aria-expanded"?: boolean | "true" | "false"
"aria-haspopup"?: boolean | "menu" | "listbox" | "tree" | "grid" | "dialog" | "true" | "false"
"aria-pressed"?: boolean | "true" | "false" | "mixed"
```
Two things here: hyphenated prop names (need string keys, already handled by BTreeMap), and `boolean | "true" | "false"` which is a WHATWG IDL compat pattern. Should display as-is — it's genuinely the interface.

### Pattern 8 — `.d.ts` component declaration (all libraries)
```ts
// Every .d.ts file
declare const Button: React.ForwardRefExoticComponent<
  ButtonProps & React.RefAttributes<HTMLButtonElement>
>
```
The extractor currently detects components via `const Button: FC<P> = ...` and `forwardRef<E,P>(...)`. But `.d.ts` files use `declare const Button: ForwardRefExoticComponent<P & RefAttributes<E>>`. This pattern is completely unhandled and affects every library fixture.

### Pattern 9 — Inline object type as prop (Mantine)
```ts
