## Caching — critical for performance

The typescript-go resolver is slow on first run (loads the whole TS program) but its results are deterministic: for the same `(file, typeExpression)` the answer never changes until a package updates. So we cache aggressively:

```rust
// Extends the DTS cache we already designed
pub struct TypeResolutionCache {
    /// (file_path, mtime_ns, file_size, type_expression) → resolved string
    entries: DashMap<TypeCacheKey, ResolvedTypeEntry>,
}

#[derive(Hash, Eq, PartialEq)]
struct TypeCacheKey {
    file_path: Utf8PathBuf,
    mtime_ns: u128,
    file_size: u64,
    type_expression: String,
}

struct ResolvedTypeEntry {
    display_string: String,       // raw from typescript-go
    prop_type: PropType,           // our parsed version
    resolved_at: u64,             // for cache debugging
}
```

**Cache hit rate in practice:** After the first run of a project, almost every Opaque type from `node_modules` is cached — those files don't change between runs. Only local project types that get modified need re-querying. This means watch mode is effectively static-only after warm-up.

