## What we parse from typescript-go's display string

typescript-go returns the type as a human-readable display string. We need a parser to turn that back into our `PropType` — essentially a mini TypeScript type expression parser. This is much simpler than parsing full TypeScript source because:
- No generics to resolve (they're already resolved in the display string)
- No imports to follow  
- No inference
- Just structural decomposition

```rust
fn parse_ts_display_string(s: &str) -> PropType {
    // "string" → PropType::String
    // "number" → PropType::Number
    // "boolean" → PropType::Boolean
    // "string | number" → PropType::Union([String, Number])
    // "string[]" → PropType::Array(String)
    // "\"outlined\" | \"filled\" | \"standard\"" → Union of StringLiterals
    // "React.CSSProperties | ((theme: Theme) => ...)" → Union
    // "{ from: string; to: string; deg?: number }" → Object([...])
    // "readonly (boolean | null)[]" → Array(Union([Boolean, Null]))
    // Anything complex → Opaque with the raw display string (still better than before)
}
```

This parser covers enough cases to be genuinely useful. Even when it can't fully parse the result, it replaces `Opaque { raw: "SxProps<Theme>" }` with `Opaque { raw: "React.CSSProperties | ((theme: Theme) => React.CSSProperties) | readonly (boolean | React.CSSProperties | null | undefined)[]" }` — which is much more informative for Storybook users.

