## What this means for our architecture

**New crate/package:** `packages/type-resolver/` — the TypeScript helper script. Ships as a dev dependency of `@oxc-react-docgen/vite`. Small: ~100 lines, depends only on `typescript`.

**New fields in `PipelineOptions`:**
```rust
pub struct PipelineOptions {
    // ...existing...
    pub resolve_complex_types: Option<ComplexTypeResolutionOptions>,
}

pub struct ComplexTypeResolutionOptions {
    pub tsconfig_path: Option<Utf8PathBuf>,
    pub engine: ResolutionEngine,  // Auto | TypeScript | TypescriptGo
    pub timeout_ms: u64,
}
```

**New `OpaqueReason` variants to mark "worth querying typescript-go":**
```rust
pub enum OpaqueReason {
    // ...existing...
    ConditionalType,   // T extends U ? X : Y — typescript-go can resolve
    MappedType,        // { [K in Keys]: V } — typescript-go can resolve
    IndexedAccess,     // Type["key"] — typescript-go can resolve
    // ...existing others stay as-is (module augmentation, runtime-dependent, etc.)
}

impl OpaqueReason {
    /// Whether typescript-go resolution should be attempted for this reason
    pub fn can_resolve_with_type_checker(&self) -> bool {
        matches!(self,
            OpaqueReason::ConditionalType |
            OpaqueReason::MappedType |
            OpaqueReason::IndexedAccess |
            OpaqueReason::DepthExceeded
        )
    }
}
```

The pipeline then collects all `Opaque` props where `can_resolve_with_type_checker()` is true, batches them, and invokes the subprocess at the end of static resolution.

---

## Performance model — vs RDT

| Scenario | RDT | Us (no typescript-go) | Us (with typescript-go) |
|----------|-----|----------------------|------------------------|
| 50 components, cold | 14-30s | <10ms | <10ms + ~200ms first run |
| 50 components, warm | 14-30s | <10ms | <10ms (cache hits) |
| Watch mode, file change | ~3-5s | <5ms | <5ms (warm LSP) |
| SxProps resolved? | ✅ (slow) | ❌ Opaque | ✅ (cached after first) |
| Conditional types? | ✅ (slow) | ❌ Opaque | ✅ (cached after first) |

The critical insight: we're **better than RDT on accuracy for complex types** once the cache is warm, and dramatically faster on every run because we only use the type checker as a targeted oracle for edge cases.

