## Testing Validation

### What we're doing right

- **`#[cfg(test)]` inline** for `pub(crate)` internals — correct; `tests/` can't reach `pub(crate)` items, so inline is the only option
- **`tests/snapshots.rs` tests the public `extract()` API** — correct boundary; integration tests use only `pub` surface
- **`insta` for JSON snapshots** — correct; `assert_json_snapshot!` over `assert_snapshot!` because JSON whitespace is normalized and diffs are readable
- **90 + 7 = 97 tests** across 6 source files

### Gaps that need fixing

#### 1. `pipeline/watch.rs` has zero tests

This is the most important gap. `WatchSession` is our most stateful, concurrent code — the `Mutex<bool>` guard we just added has no test proving it works.

We need an inline `#[cfg(test)]` module in `watch.rs`:

```rust
#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;
    use std::sync::Arc;
    use std::thread;

    fn empty_options() -> PipelineOptions {
        PipelineOptions { src_dirs: vec![], ..Default::default() }
    }

    #[test]
    fn initialize_is_idempotent() {
        let session = WatchSession::new(empty_options());
        let first = session.initialize();
        let second = session.initialize();
        assert_eq!(first.components.len(), second.components.len());
    }

    #[test]
    fn initialize_concurrent_guard() {
        let session = Arc::new(WatchSession::new(empty_options()));
        let handles: Vec<_> = (0..8)
            .map(|_| {
                let s = Arc::clone(&session);
                thread::spawn(move || s.initialize())
            })
            .collect();
        let results: Vec<_> = handles.into_iter().map(|h| h.join().unwrap()).collect();
        // All concurrent callers must return the same component count
        let count = results[0].components.len();
        assert!(results.iter().all(|r| r.components.len() == count));
    }

    #[test]
    fn snapshot_returns_current_cache() {
        let session = WatchSession::new(empty_options());
        session.initialize();
        let snap = session.snapshot();
        assert!(snap.diagnostics.is_empty());
    }
}
```

**Why `std::thread::spawn` and not `tokio::test`**: `WatchSession` is purely synchronous. The concurrency comes from rayon internally and from multiple threads calling into it. `std::thread::spawn` is the right tool — `tokio::test` is for async code. Don't add a tokio dep to core just for tests.

#### 2. `cdylib`-only NAPI crate can't run inline tests

`crates/napi/Cargo.toml` has `crate-type = ["cdylib"]`. A `cdylib` produces a shared library but no test binary — `cargo test -p oxc-react-docgen-napi` silently runs nothing.

The fix is to also expose `rlib`:

```toml
[lib]
crate-type = ["cdylib", "rlib"]
```

With `rlib` present, `cargo test` compiles a test binary. The NAPI types compile fine in that context — the `#[napi]` attributes are conditionally active only when `napi` feature is present, but the underlying Rust types and `From` impls are always there.

This unlocks testing `From<JsExtractOptions> for PipelineOptions` in Rust:

```rust
